    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-51343",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c514865c-02d0-5d59-938b-fdd739cdb257",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Velocity Vector Given the Acceleration Vector",
      "problem": "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},{e}^{3t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨{e}^{2t},{e}^{3t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1)}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3(1)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩ & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}⟩\\,.\n$$",
