      "math_academy_id": "q-31628",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector",
      "problem": "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨8{t}^{3},-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,\\,3⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=3.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8{t}^{3},-1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int -1\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨-1,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨2(1)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,3⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},\\,-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-3$ and ${C}_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨2{t}^{4}-3,\\,-t+4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨2(3)^{4}-3,\\,-(3)+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨159,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
