      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector",
      "problem": "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨12(1+2t)^{3},9(1-3t)^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,6⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨12(1+2t)^{3},9(1-3t)^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 12(1+2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 9(1-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨1,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2(0))^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3(0))^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,6⟩ & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}+{C}_{1},\\,-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${C}_{2}=7.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}(1+2(1))^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3(1))^{3}+7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨121,15⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
